{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>And it shall be forgiven them.</b> That the language of forgiveness was not used for the anointed priest, in the manner that the language of forgiveness was mentioned for the congregation, the ruler, and the individual — the Ramban explained that because of the great eminence of the priest, he is not completely atoned, for 'the angel of YHWH of hosts' is he (Malachi 2:7), and his iniquity is greater than can be borne, until he prays and supplicates his Elohim. And Mahariv wrote that what is said regarding the sin-offering of the congregation, 'and it shall be forgiven them,' refers to the priest and to the congregation, including them together, since both of them sinned in a matter of legal ruling, for they had permitted the forbidden." "And it appears to me that this is why forgiveness is not mentioned for the anointed priest — because for anyone who causes the multitude to sin, the sin of the multitude hangs upon him. And regarding the anointed priest it is written, 'if the anointed priest sin to the guilt of the people' — Rashi explained that the guilt of the people is his sin, for they depend on him to make atonement for them and he became flawed. And it is more correct to explain 'to the guilt of the people' that he falls under the category of one who causes the multitude to sin, for from him they will see and so they will act, by a fortiori reasoning. Therefore 'and it shall be forgiven him' is not said of him, for even though the sacrifice may suffice to remove his sin from upon him and his iniquity departs, nevertheless the impression of the sin of the multitude that hangs upon him still remains, and therefore there is no forgiveness. And do not answer me from the fact that the language of forgiveness is said regarding the ruler who sins — that is because others do not learn from him at all, and on the contrary, the sin is attributed to his exalted position, to say that because he conducted his high office with arrogance, therefore he came to sin — for everyone knows that haughtiness of spirit in a man leads him to transgress the will of his Maker. And if so, they do not learn the sin from him; and on the contrary, they learn from him the way of repentance, as it is written 'when a ruler sins' — Rashi explained: fortunate is the generation, etc." "And as for the fact that the language of forgiveness is said in the section 'if the whole congregation of Israel err' — whichever way you look at it: if the section speaks of a complete congregation that all of them literally sinned, then there are no 'causers of the multitude to sin' here, for they all sinned as one. And if the section speaks of the Sanhedrin, as they derived from a gezerah shavah of 'congregation, congregation' — if so, the congregation did not sin at all, for the Holy One, blessed be He, had already commanded them that they should obey the voice of their instructors, and if they sinned and the instructors ruled in error, what did these sheep do? And if so, there are no 'causers of the multitude to sin' here either. And if you wish to say that this is still called 'causers of the multitude to sin,' we can say that this is why it concludes and says 'it is a sin-offering of the congregation' — to say that even though 'and it shall be forgiven them' is said, nevertheless this is not [complete] forgiveness, for 'it is a sin-offering of the congregation' — and that impression remains standing. It is as though it said that everything is forgiven them except for that which is 'a sin-offering of the congregation' — that sin remains standing. And all of this is a great warning to instructors of legal rulings and to the great ones of Israel, that they must be exceedingly careful in their deeds, for the inadvertence of a teacher is accounted as deliberate — for what he does inadvertently causes that from him they will see and so they will do deliberately, for they will not know that it is an error on their part."], :book :leviticus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:35:30.130347105Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}